<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Guelph Sidewalks &mdash; Reference Layer for OSM Editors</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="index.css">
<link rel="stylesheet"
      href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css"
      integrity="sha256-p4NxAoJBhIIN+hmNHrzRCf9tD/miZyoHS5obTRR9BMY="
      crossorigin="">
<script defer src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"
        integrity="sha256-20nQCchB9co0qIjJZRGuk2/Z9VM+kNiyxNV1lvTlZBo="
        crossorigin=""></script>
<script defer src="index.js"></script>
<script data-goatcounter="https://osm-guelph.goatcounter.com/count" async src="//gc.zgo.at/count.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
<main>

<h1>Guelph Sidewalks</h1>
<p class="lead">
  A reference layer of the sidewalks published by the City of Guelph &mdash;
  <strong>{{LINE_COUNT}}</strong> lines from the City's Sidewalks layer,
  <strong>{{BUILT_KM}} km</strong> of them built &mdash; ready to add to the
  <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org">OpenStreetMap</a> editors
  <strong>iD</strong> and <strong>JOSM</strong> while you map.
</p>
<p class="meta">City data: {{DATA_DATE}} &middot; tiles rebuilt: {{BUILD_DATE}}</p>

<div class="attribution">
  <a href="{{LICENSE_URL}}">{{LICENSE_NOTICE}}</a>
  Source: <a href="{{DATASET_PAGE}}">Sidewalks</a> (OpenData2/FeatureServer/0).
</div>

<h2>Preview</h2>
<div id="preview-map" data-raster-url="{{RASTER_URL}}"
     data-minzoom="{{RASTER_MINZOOM}}" data-maxzoom="{{RASTER_MAXZOOM}}"></div>
<p class="meta">
  The raster layer drawn over OpenStreetMap, from zoom {{RASTER_MINZOOM}}:
  <span style="color:#15aabf">cyan</span> lines are built sidewalks,
  <span style="color:#e8590c">orange</span> ones proposed.
</p>

<div class="attribution">
  <strong>Coverage vs OpenStreetMap:</strong>
  <a href="gaps/">{{UNTRACED_KM}} km of City sidewalk</a> has no OSM line
  within {{MATCH_M}}&nbsp;m &mdash; a mapper's worklist, street by street,
  with an interactive map.
</div>

<h2>Two layers &mdash; which to use</h2>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Vector layer</th>
    <td>Interactive. Click a line to read its tags (layer
        <code>{{VECTOR_LAYER_NAME}}</code>: <code>id</code>,
        <code>status</code>, <code>surface</code>, <code>width</code> in m,
        <code>installed</code> year, <code>length</code> in m). Zooms
        {{VECTOR_MINZOOM}}&ndash;{{VECTOR_MAXZOOM}}. Best in
        <strong>iD</strong>.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <th>Raster layer</th>
    <td>Lines drawn directly on the tiles. A tracing backdrop over aerial
        imagery. Best in <strong>JOSM</strong>.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2>Add to iD</h2>
<h3>Vector layer (interactive)</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Open the iD editor and press <kbd>U</kbd> for the <em>Map Data</em> panel.</li>
  <li>Under <em>Custom Map Data</em>, open the settings and paste this URL:</li>
</ol>
<pre class="url">{{VECTOR_URL}}</pre>
<p>Zoom in, then click any line to see its tags in the inspector.</p>

<h3>Raster layer</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Open the <em>Background</em> panel and choose <em>Custom</em>.</li>
  <li>Paste this URL template:</li>
</ol>
<pre class="url">{{RASTER_URL}}</pre>
<p>
  As a background it replaces the imagery; the vector layer is the one to
  use over imagery in iD.
</p>

<h2>Add to JOSM</h2>
<h3>Raster layer (recommended for JOSM)</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Open <em>Preferences &rarr; Imagery</em> and click <strong>+TMS</strong>.</li>
  <li>Paste this entry (the <code>tms[{{RASTER_MINZOOM}},{{RASTER_MAXZOOM}}]</code>
      prefix sets the zoom range; JOSM scales it up past z{{RASTER_MAXZOOM}}):</li>
</ol>
<pre class="url">tms[{{RASTER_MINZOOM}},{{RASTER_MAXZOOM}}]:{{RASTER_URL_JOSM}}</pre>
<p>
  Give it a name such as <em>Guelph Sidewalks</em>, click OK, then enable it
  from the <em>Imagery</em> menu above your aerial imagery. Note JOSM's
  placeholder is <code>{zoom}</code>, not <code>{z}</code>.
</p>

<h3>Vector layer (optional)</h3>
<p>
  JOSM can also add the vector tiles via <em>Preferences &rarr; Imagery</em> &rarr;
  <strong>+MVT</strong>:
</p>
<pre class="url">{{VECTOR_URL_JOSM}}</pre>

<h2>Notes</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Guelph maps its sidewalks as separate ways
      (<code>highway=footway</code> + <code>footway=sidewalk</code>), not as
      <code>sidewalk=*</code> tags on the road, so tracing from this layer
      follows the local convention. Where a road already carries
      <code>sidewalk=*</code>, that is a valid way to map it too.</li>
  <li>Tracing a sidewalk at a time from imagery, with this layer as a guide,
      is ordinary mapping. Copying the City's lines into OSM by script would
      be an import, and needs an import proposal and community discussion
      first.</li>
  <li>The City states its sidewalks are accurate to 1&ndash;5 m; the imagery
      decides where a line goes. Proposed sidewalks (orange) may not have
      been poured yet.</li>
  <li>Vector tiles are pre-rendered through zoom {{VECTOR_MAXZOOM}}; raster
      tiles for z{{RASTER_MINZOOM}}&ndash;{{RASTER_MAXZOOM}} (the editor
      overzooms them further).</li>
  <li>Tiles are refreshed weekly from the City's service.</li>
  <li>This is a <strong>reference</strong> layer &mdash; check it against
      current imagery before copying anything into OpenStreetMap. Where a
      sidewalk in OSM already exists, improve it in place rather than
      replacing it, so its history and tags survive.</li>
</ul>

